  "answer_text": "The whitish film developing on your glass is caused by one of two very common culprits (or a combination of both): **mineral deposits (limescale)** or a **bacterial biofilm**. \n\nBecause you are using filtered fridge water and letting the glass sit for about a week, there are scientific reasons why both of these things can happen, even if the water seems perfectly clean.\n\n---\n\n### Culprit 1: Mineral Buildup (Limescale)\nEven though your water is filtered, most standard refrigerator filters (usually activated carbon filters) are designed to remove chlorine, heavy metals, and bad tastes/odors. **They do not remove dissolved minerals like calcium and magnesium**. \n\n* **How it happens:** Every time you take a sip or empty the glass, a thin layer of water is left on the inner walls. As that water evaporates into the air, it leaves behind those dissolved minerals. Over the course of a week, as you refill the glass and the water level fluctuates, these microscopic mineral layers build up on top of one another, forming a visible, chalky white haze or ring.\n* **How to test for it:** Pour a splash of white vinegar into the glass and swirl it around, or wipe the film with a vinegar-soaked cloth. If the white film dissolves and disappears almost instantly, it is mineral buildup.\n\n### Culprit 2: Microbial Biofilm\nIf the film is more \"slippery\" than \"chalky,\" you are likely dealing with a biofilm. \n\n* **How it happens:** Your mouth naturally contains millions of harmless bacteria and yeast. Every time you take a sip, some of these microbes\u2014along with microscopic food particles and saliva\u2014are backwashed into the glass. Because fridge filters remove chlorine (which is tap water's built-in sanitizer), there is nothing in the water to stop these microbes from multiplying. Over several days, the bacteria cling to the wet glass and secrete a slimy, protective matrix to anchor themselves. This structure is called a biofilm, and it can look like a cloudy, whitish, or translucent film.\n* **How to test for it:** If the film feels slightly slimy when wet, has a faint musty or \"stale water\" smell, or requires a bit of dish soap and a sponge to fully wash away, it is a biofilm.\n\n---\n\n### How to Prevent It\n1. **Wash with soap daily:** Simply rinsing a glass with water does not break up a biofilm. You need the friction of a sponge and dish soap to tear down the bacterial matrix. Try to wash your glass every 1 to 2 days.\n2. **Use a vinegar rinse:** If you have hard water, soak the glass in a mixture of warm water and vinegar for 15 minutes once a week to prevent the minerals from etching or permanently clouding the glass.\n3. **Let it dry completely:** Bacteria need moisture to thrive. Letting your glass dry completely between uses will halt biofilm growth.",
